    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors like Lisinopril? I'm specifically trying to understand how they interact with the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) to reduce blood pressure.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lisinopril and other ACE inhibitors work by blocking the enzyme angiotensin-converting enzyme, which is responsible for converting angiotensin I into the potent vasoconstrictor angiotensin II. By preventing this conversion, the drug causes a reduction in peripheral vascular resistance and decreases aldosterone secretion, leading to reduced salt and water retention. This dual action results in overall vasodilation and a subsequent lowering of blood pressure without altering the heart's inotropic function.",
      "source": "target",
