    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the RAAS pathway itself? I want to make sure I understand the step-by-step sequence of how renin starts the whole process and how the different hormones interact before the ACE step happens.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System, the process begins when the kidneys release renin in response to low blood pressure or low sodium. Renin then cleaves the amino acid chain from the inactive protein angiotensinogen to form angiotensin I, a relatively weak decapeptide. Subsequently, endothelial cells activated by endothelin release angiotensin II, the most active decapeptide of the system. Angiotensin II directly constricts the arteries while stimulating the adrenal cortex to release aldosterone, which increases sodium and water absorption back into the body.",
      "source": "target",
